the opportunity to discuss the cases of erroneous identifications of some 
Creto-Mycenaean gold rings as fakes . This tendency, underpinned by the obscure 
– even legendary – circumstances in which these rings were found, not only the 
Ring of Nestor but also the Ring of Minos, has been reassessed completely in 
favour of their authenticity. This revision has been significantly reinforced by
recent finds of iconographic subjects of comparable resonance (wall-paintings, 
gold rings, figurines, ivories, and so on), as well as by more thorough study of
the techniques and craftsmanship of these unique signet rings of the first 
centuries of the Late Bronze Age (16th-15th century BC). The key pictorial unit 
of the Ring of Nestor examined in this paper is Evans’s “watch-dog” or “dragon”,
namely the guardian of the old “tree of the world”, whose branches host the most
populous scene in Aegean glyptic art. This motif is here identified as a 
scorpion, a major apotropaic symbol in Bronze Αge Aegean iconography, which 
kills the “Μinoan Dragon” of the ring and offers a sound interpretation of the 
ring’s iconography.\n\n## Key takeaways sparkles AI\n\n1. The Ring of Nestor, 
measuring 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm, is the fifth largest Aegean signet ring.\n2. 
Research confirms its authenticity, contrasting earlier claims of forgery by 
scholars in the 1920s.\n3. The scorpion, identified as a significant apotropaic 
symbol, plays a crucial role in the ring's iconography.\n4. Recent findings 
enhance the understanding of Aegean glyptic art from the Late Bronze Age 
(16th-15th century BC).\n5. This text aims to document the authenticity of the 
Ring of Nestor and reassess historical misconceptions.\n\n## Related 
