[tools:d70b3c26-ecf2-e942-0ba7-44daac044b91] [AIMessage] Below is a curated set 
of non‑Wikipedia sources about the “Ring of Nestor”, focused on discovery 
context, early publication, description, dating, iconography, provenance, and 
authenticity debates.

I have **not** synthesized these into a narrative article (since you asked 
specifically for source-finding and grounding). Each entry includes: 
bibliographic info, URL, type, relevance, and a faithful summary of what can be 
seen in the accessible text (no invented metadata).

---

### 1. Vlachopoulos, A. G. 2020 – Detailed monograph on iconography and 
authenticity

**Title**: “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for authenticity”  
**In**: F. Blakolmer (ed.), *Current Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean 
Iconography*, Aegis 18, 2020, pp. 223–252  
**Author / Institution**: Andreas G. Vlachopoulos; Aegis series (Université 
catholique de Louvain / Belgian School at Athens context)  
**URL**:  
- Academia.edu entry (PDF): 
https://www.academia.edu/43416584/_2020_A_G_Vlachopoulos_The_Ring_of_Nestor_and_
the_quest_for_authenticity_in_F_Blakolmer_ed_Current_Approaches_and_New_Perspect
ives_in_Aegean_Iconography_Aegis_18_2020_pp_223_252  
**Type**: Scholarly book chapter (peer‑reviewed conference volume)  
**Query that triggered it**: “Ring of Nestor” gold ring archaeology discovery 
context excavation publication -site:en.wikipedia.org  
**Relevance**: Central, up‑to‑date scholarly synthesis on the ring’s 
**authentication, iconography, stylistic/technical analysis, and 
historiography** of the debate (including earlier publications).  

**Content / useful points (from visible text)**:
- Abstract explicitly states that the study documents the **authenticity** of 
the so‑called *Ring of Nestor* (CMS VI, no. 277).
- It notes that earlier scholarship (1920s and later) had marked some gold 
rings, including the Ring of Nestor and Ring of Minos, as **possible fakes**, 
but recent work has “reassessed completely in favour of their authenticity.”
- Cites new comparative **iconographic parallels** (wall paintings, gold rings, 
figurines, ivories) and detailed **technical and craftsmanship analysis** for 
rings of the early Late Bronze Age (16th–15th c. BC).
- Gives dimensions: the ring is ca. **3.29 × 2.18 cm**, and is characterized as 
the **fifth largest Aegean signet ring**.
- Discusses Arthur **Evans’s description** of one motif on the ring as a 
“watch-dog” or “dragon” guarding the “tree of the world”; Vlachopoulos 
re‑interprets this as a **scorpion**, an apotropaic symbol in Aegean Bronze Age 
iconography, important for your iconography and meaning section.
- The article’s long literature review (not visible in full text here but 
indicated by notes and references) is likely to list:
  - **original or early publications of the ring**,
  - prior authenticity challenges,
  - technical analyses (including composition; the Serper snippet notes 97% gold
content).

For your purposes, this is a **key modern reference** for:
- a neutral summary of authenticity debates;
- description and size;  
- iconographic interpretation;  
- the ring’s place in Aegean glyptic and funerary/ritual imagery.

---

### 2. Eliopoulos, T. 2013 – Iconography and authenticity in an Aegean forgeries
context

**Title**: “SOME OBSERVATIONS ON THE ICONOGRAPHY OF THE ‘RING OF NESTOR’”  
**Journal**: *Annuario della Scuola Archeologica di Atene e delle Missioni 
Italiane in Oriente* (annual of the Italian School at Athens)  
**Author**: Theodore Eliopoulos  
**URL**:  
- Academia.edu entry: 
https://www.academia.edu/83658257/SOME_OBSERVATIONS_ON_THE_ICONOGRAPHY_OF_THE_RI
NG_OF_NESTOR_  
**Type**: Peer‑reviewed journal article  
**Query**: Same as above  
**Relevance**: Focused on **iconography** and **authenticity**; situates the 
ring within Aegean forgery debates and detailed motif analysis.

**Content / useful points**:
- Abstract describes the paper as an **analysis of the iconography** of the Ring
of Nestor in the context of **Aegean forgeries and authenticity debates**, 
aiming to contribute to discourse on its significance for Minoan and Mycenaean 
art.
- Emphasizes:
  - the use of **iconographic analysis** to test authenticity (comparing motifs,
altars, trees, divine/ritual scenes);
  - **“connection between incurved altars and the ring’s depiction”** as 
supporting authenticity (useful for your description and religious/contextual 
interpretation);
  - highlights that **generations of scholars** have debated the genuineness of 
both the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos.
- This article likely revisits earlier key discussions (Nilsson, Biesantz, 
Xenaki‑Sakellariou, Pini, etc.) and provides detailed motif readings.

This is useful for:
- a balanced section on **iconography** (altars, sacred trees, animals, 
worshippers, etc.);
- mapping **scholarly positions on authenticity** and iconographic 
consistency/inconsistency with securely excavated rings.

