underworld or afterlife**, emphasizing connections with **Egyptian religious 
imagery** (e.g., weighing of souls, boat journey of the dead, etc.), and uses 
comparative iconography to support this.

- **Interpretative framework**:  
  - He argues that the ring’s imagery offers a rare glimpse into Minoan beliefs 
about the **after-world**, and he places the ring within a broader corpus of 
Minoan and Mycenaean signet rings depicting ritual scenes.  
  - Evans assumes the ring is ancient and authentic, Late Minoan in date, and 
produced on Crete before ending up in a mainland tomb.

