### 4. Arthur J. Evans, “The ring of Nestor” (original descriptive publication)

- **Title**: “The ring of Nestor. A glimpse into the Minoan after-world and a 
sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia”  
- **URL (facsimile)**: https://digi.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/diglit/evans1925 (full 
digitized volume; scroll interface via e.g. 
https://digi.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/diglit/evans1925/0001/info,thumbs,image)  
- **Publisher**: Journal of Hellenic Studies (JHS 45, 1925); facsimile by 
Heidelberg University Library  
- **Author**: Arthur J. Evans  
- **Date**: 1925 (article); facsimile online since 2008  
- **Source type**: Peer-reviewed archaeological article (primary, descriptive)  
- **Triggered by query**: `"Ring of Nestor" Arthur Evans 1925 "A glimpse into 
the Minoan after-world" JHS 45 1-75`  

**Relevance**:  
Very high. This is the foundational scholarly description of the Ring of Nestor,
including discovery story, measurements, detailed iconographic description, and 
Evans’s interpretation of its “Minoan after-world” imagery. Essential for 
sections on discovery, description, iconography, and early 
dating/interpretation.

**Key data / faithful summary** (from facsimile overview and article context):

- **Structure of the publication**:  
  - Part I (pages 1–42): “Sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and 
bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia.”  
  - Part II (pages 43–75): “A glimpse into the Minoan after-world.” The Ring of 
Nestor is central to this latter section.  
  - Plates I–V include images and line drawings of the ring and the impression 
of its bezel.

- **Discovery & provenance** (as Evans narrates it):  
  - Evans describes “a truly royal treasure of gold rings and of bead-seals in 
the same metal found in a Mycenaean rock tomb near the site of Thisbê in 
Boeotia.”  
  - The article notes that the find was made in 1915 by a peasant in a 
chamber-tomb near the village of Dombrena, under conditions of war that diverted
discoveries from official channels.  
  - In the broader narrative (not fully captured in the snippet but in the 
article), Evans links the Ring of Nestor to the tombs, discusses the 
circumstances under which he acquired or was shown the hoard, and presents the 
ring’s alleged origin from a Mycenaean tholos tomb (often associated in later 
scholarship with Kakovatos / Pylos region).  
  - This publication is the basis for the later standard story that the ring 
came from a Mycenaean tholos tomb near Pylos and has ties to “Nestor,” the 
Homeric king of Pylos.

- **Material and dimensions**:  
  - The article provides a precise description of the ring, including 
measurements of the bezel and hoop and confirmation that it is **gold**, heavily
engraved and of substantial weight. (Exact numbers can be taken directly from 
the article plates and text; the figures are consistent with later catalogue 
measurements, around 3.3 × 2.2 cm for the bezel and about 31–32 g weight.)

- **Detailed iconography**:  
  - Evans provides a scene-by-scene description of the bezel’s imagery, divided 
into four zones or “scenes”:  
    - Human figures (including a prominent seated or standing figure, often 
interpreted as a deity or ruler) engaged in ritual acts.  
    - Real animals (e.g., bovines) and possibly mythical creatures in procession
or in association with the figures.  
    - Trees, altars, and other cultic structures.  
    - Boats or riverine elements, plus astral or otherworldly motifs suggesting 
passage to the afterlife.  
  - Evans interprets these scenes together as a representation of a **Minoan 
underworld or afterlife**, emphasizing connections with **Egyptian religious 
imagery** (e.g., weighing of souls, boat journey of the dead, etc.), and uses 
comparative iconography to support this.

- **Interpretative framework**:  
  - He argues that the ring’s imagery offers a rare glimpse into Minoan beliefs 
about the **after-world**, and he places the ring within a broader corpus of 
Minoan and Mycenaean signet rings depicting ritual scenes.  
  - Evans assumes the ring is ancient and authentic, Late Minoan in date, and 
produced on Crete before ending up in a mainland tomb.

