      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "Andreas G. Vlachopoulos’s 2020 paper \"The Ring of 
Nestor and the quest for authenticity\" argues, on the basis of long-term 
research, that the Ring of Nestor (CMS VI no. 277) is an authentic Late Bronze 
Age Aegean gold signet ring rather than a modern forgery. The abstract states 
that the study documents the ring’s authenticity and reassesses earlier 
scholarly tendencies to label certain Creto-Mycenaean gold rings, including the 
Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos, as fakes because of the obscure or 
legendary circumstances of their discovery. This revision towards authenticity 
is said to be strengthened by recent finds of iconographic subjects comparable 
to those on these rings (wall paintings, gold rings, figurines, ivories) and by 
detailed study of their techniques and craftsmanship. A key part of the paper is
a stylistic and iconographic re-interpretation of the main pictorial motif on 
the Ring of Nestor: what Arthur Evans described as a \"watch-dog\" or \"dragon\"
guarding a \"tree of the world\" is re-identified by Vlachopoulos as a scorpion,
an apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age Aegean iconography, which \"kills the Minoan 
Dragon\" and allows a coherent reading of the ring’s imagery.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
