      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "This 2020 paper by A. G. Vlachopoulos discusses the 
so‑called Ring of Nestor (CMS VI, no. 277), focusing on its authenticity and 
iconography in the wider context of Aegean Bronze Age glyptic. The author 
describes years of research that document the ring’s authenticity and reassess 
earlier scholarly tendencies to regard the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos 
as potential forgeries, a tendency linked to the obscure or legendary 
circumstances of their discovery. The paper argues that recent finds of 
comparable iconographic motifs in securely excavated contexts, together with 
detailed study of techniques and craftsmanship, strongly support the 
authenticity of these unique gold signet rings from the early Late Bronze Age 
(16th–15th century BC). It examines the key pictorial unit on the Ring of 
Nestor, originally termed Evans’s “watch‑dog” or “dragon” guarding an old ‘tree 
of the world’, and reinterprets this figure as a scorpion, understood as a major
apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age Aegean iconography. The paper notes the ring’s 
size (3.29 × 2.18 cm) and identifies it as the fifth largest known Aegean signet
ring, emphasizing its high craftsmanship and its importance for understanding 
Minoan and Mycenaean ideology.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
