broader argument, the author reconstructs the historiography of the Ring of 
Nestor as a case study, outlining how scholarly opinion on its authenticity has 
shifted over time. Martin Nilsson originally treated the ring as an authentic 
Bronze Age artifact but, in the second edition of his ‘Minoan‑Mycenaean 
Religion’ (1950), reclassified it among suspect objects. Hagen Biesantz in 1954 
argued decisively against the ring’s authenticity based on stylistic 
peculiarities. Agni Xenaki‑Sakellariou in 1994 examined iconographic, 
typological, and technical aspects she considered suspicious. Ingo Pini’s 1998 
article responded to this critique by presenting new observations and evidence 
in support of the ring’s authenticity, referencing more recent comparative 
